			<div class="content" id="post-content-2761659">
									

<p>Awkward phrasing:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Currently, of all the survivors4 of SCP-2861's effects, Mr. Jacob Van Dijk (34 years old at time of recovery) has been deemed the most lucid.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Better as &quot;Of all current survivors, Mr Jacob&#8230;&quot;</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Schizotypal personality disorder</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Oh. :| You might also want to put the intense social anxiety, too. That's another defining trait. Unusual behavior, unusual perceptions, intense social anxiety that does not fade with time.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Thus far, no common or Foundation-developed treatments have permanently improved the mental health of SCP-2861 victims.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Schizotypal personality disorder does not have permanent improvements. It's a chronic condition with no cure.</p>
<p>Overall, am I&#8230; missing something? People appear in graveyards, some panic, others kind of enjoyed it and develop symptoms of schizotypal personality disorder but are otherwise normal? I am concerned I'm missing the broader implications here. This appears to me to be just a thing which makes people mentally ill and appear in strange ways, and that mental illness is anomalous in a strange way.</p>
<p>If the hotel aspect was emphasized more, this may be interesting, but right now I'm vaguely confused at what the underlying idea really is, or why it's significant. For now it is interesting, but I'm left vaguely confused and unsure what the takeaway is. If there are underlying themes, they should be brought forth harder than they are now, and stitched together better. They seem a little disparate now.</p>

							</div>

										<div class="signature">
					<hr class="signature-separator"/>
					

<p>Living the dream, or dreaming the life?</p>

